{
  "dom_id": "step-q353464",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Iodine-131 has a half-life of $8.02$ days. A sample of iodine-131 has an initial mass of $7.2\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$ Rounded to **one decimal place**, what is the mass of the sample after $12$ days?\n\nThe mass of the sample after $12$ days is approximately\n{{field-1}}\n$\\mu \\text{g}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance at time $t$ can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n{M}^{\u2032}(t)=-kM(t),\\,M(0)={M}_{0}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nM(t)={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life ${t}_{1/2}$ is the time taken for the mass of the substance to reduce by one half and is given by\n\n$$\n{t}_{1/2}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{k}.\n$$\n\nSince we know that ${t}_{1/2}=8.02$ days, we have\n\n$$\n8.02=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{k}\\,\u21d2\\,k=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}{8.02}.\n$$\n\nAlso, since ${M}_{0}=7.2\\,\\mu \\text{g},$ we have the particular solution\n\n$$\nM(t)=7.2{e}^{-t\\operatorname{ln}\u20612/8.02}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nM(12)=7.2{e}^{-12\\operatorname{ln}\u20612/8.02}\u22482.6,\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the mass of the sample remaining after $12$ days is approximately $2.6\\,\\mu \\text{g}.$",
